__d(function(e,t,a,n,o,i,s){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:13,chapter_title:"Fun with Magnets",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Describe the history and discovery of magnets","Identify magnetic and non-magnetic materials with examples","Explain the properties of a bar magnet \u2014 poles, attraction, repulsion","Understand how a freely suspended magnet always points north\u2013south","Describe how to find the poles of a magnet using another magnet","Explain how a compass works and its use in navigation","Describe methods of making a temporary magnet","Understand how magnets lose their magnetic properties","Connect magnetism to everyday technology and Karnataka examples"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Basic awareness of metals and non-metals from everyday life","Understanding that some objects attract each other (e.g. sticky tape)","Familiarity with the concept of force from daily experience"],introduction:{why_important:"Magnets are everywhere \u2014 in speakers, MRI machines, electric motors, compasses, and refrigerator doors. Understanding magnetism is the foundation for understanding electricity and many modern technologies. It is one of the four fundamental forces in nature.",real_life_relevance:"Every time you stick a note on a refrigerator, use a speaker, or check direction on your phone, you are using magnetism. Electric motors in fans, pumps, and vehicles all work because of magnetic principles.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka has significant iron ore deposits in Bellary (now Ballari) and Chitradurga districts \u2014 iron being the classic magnetic material. NMDC (National Mineral Development Corporation), headquartered in Hyderabad but with major operations in Karnataka, mines iron ore that is used in steel making. The Indian Institute of Science (IISc) in Bengaluru conducts world-class research in materials science and magnetism. ISRO's satellite guidance systems in Bengaluru use magnetic sensors. Belagavi and Hubballi have industrial units manufacturing electromagnets and motors.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Students have learned about materials and their properties in earlier chapters. This chapter extends that by exploring a special property \u2014 magnetism \u2014 shown by certain materials."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Magnet",role:"The central object \u2014 a material that attracts iron, nickel, and cobalt and has two poles"},{name:"Magnetic Needle / Compass",role:"A freely suspended magnet that always aligns north\u2013south \u2014 used for navigation for centuries"},{name:"Magnetic and Non-magnetic Materials",role:"The two categories of materials \u2014 those attracted by magnets and those not affected"}],plot:["The chapter opens with the story of Magnes, a shepherd from ancient Greece, whose iron-tipped staff stuck to a rock (lodestone/magnetite) \u2014 the legendary discovery of magnetism.","Students explore which materials are attracted by a magnet (magnetic: iron, nickel, cobalt) and which are not (non-magnetic: wood, plastic, rubber, glass, aluminium).","The chapter introduces the concept of magnetic poles \u2014 the two ends of a magnet (North and South poles) where the magnetic force is strongest.","It explains the law of magnetic poles: like poles repel, unlike poles attract.","A freely suspended bar magnet always aligns in the north\u2013south direction \u2014 this is used in compasses for navigation.","Students learn how to find the poles of an unmarked magnet using a known magnet.","The chapter covers how to make temporary magnets by stroking with a magnet or using electricity (electromagnets).","It explains how magnets lose their properties when heated, dropped, or hammered."],moral:"Simple natural phenomena like magnetism, when understood deeply, power the technologies that run our modern world."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Magnetic and Non-magnetic Materials",simple_explanation:"Some materials are attracted to magnets (magnetic materials) and some are not (non-magnetic materials). Only a few metals are magnetic.",detailed_explanation:"Magnetic materials are those attracted to a magnet. The main magnetic materials are iron, nickel, and cobalt (and their alloys like steel). Non-magnetic materials include wood, plastic, rubber, glass, paper, copper, aluminium, and most non-metals. A common misconception is that all metals are magnetic \u2014 aluminium, copper, gold, and silver are metals but are non-magnetic. The test for magnetism is simple: bring a magnet near the object. If the object is attracted, it is magnetic.",real_life_example:"Test objects in your pencil box: a steel geometry box lid will be attracted to a magnet, but an aluminium ruler will not. A steel pin will stick to a magnet, but a copper coin will not.",karnataka_example:"Iron ore mines in Ballari (formerly Bellary) district of Karnataka produce magnetite (Fe\u2083O\u2084) \u2014 a naturally occurring magnetic rock also known as lodestone. These deposits are among the largest in India. Steel plants using Karnataka iron ore produce steel \u2014 a magnetic material used in everything from utensils to bridges.",common_mistakes:"Students assume all metals are magnetic. Aluminium, copper, zinc, and gold are metals but are NOT magnetic. Only iron, nickel, cobalt and their alloys are strongly magnetic.",memory_tip:"INC = Iron, Nickel, Cobalt \u2014 the only three purely magnetic elements. Everything else is non-magnetic. IRON rules magnetism."},{concept_name:"Poles of a Magnet",simple_explanation:"Every magnet has two poles \u2014 North (N) and South (S) \u2014 where the magnetic force is strongest. Like poles repel each other; unlike poles attract.",detailed_explanation:"The magnetic force of a bar magnet is strongest at its two ends, called poles. These are labelled North (N) and South (S). The North pole of a freely suspended magnet points towards geographic north; the South pole points south. Key law: like poles (N\u2013N or S\u2013S) repel each other, while unlike poles (N\u2013S) attract. This is similar to electric charges (+ attracts \u2013). A magnet's poles cannot be separated \u2014 if you cut a magnet in half, each half becomes a new complete magnet with both N and S poles (monopoles do not exist in classical physics).",real_life_example:"If you try to push the N poles of two bar magnets together, they push away from each other (repulsion). But if you bring the N pole of one near the S pole of another, they snap together (attraction). This is visible with toy magnetic building blocks.",karnataka_example:"Magnetic levitation trains (Maglev) use repulsion between like poles to float above the track. ISRO Bengaluru uses precise magnetic sensors in satellite attitude control \u2014 these sensors rely on knowing exact pole orientations.",common_mistakes:"Students confuse attraction with the rule \u2014 they think ALL magnets attract each other. In reality, two N poles or two S poles push each other away. Attraction only occurs between N and S poles.",memory_tip:"POLES rule = Opposite Likes Enemies, Same = Enemies. N\u2013S = best friends (attraction). N\u2013N or S\u2013S = enemies (repulsion). Just like people \u2014 opposites attract!"},{concept_name:"Magnet as a Compass \u2014 Direction Finding",simple_explanation:"A freely suspended magnet always points in the north\u2013south direction. This property is used in a compass to find directions.",detailed_explanation:"When a bar magnet is suspended freely (hung from a thread or floated on water), it rotates until it aligns with Earth's magnetic field \u2014 North pole pointing approximately towards geographic north, South pole towards geographic south. This is because Earth itself acts like a giant magnet with magnetic poles near the geographic poles. A compass is simply a lightweight magnetised needle pivoted at its centre so it can rotate freely. The needle always points north, helping navigators find directions. Sailors and trekkers have used magnetic compasses for over a thousand years. Modern smartphones have electronic compasses (magnetometers) built in.",real_life_example:"If you are lost in a forest and have a compass, the needle always points north. You then know which direction is east, west, and south by using north as your reference. Indian Railways and shipping use compasses for navigation even today.",karnataka_example:"The Karnataka Forest Department and wildlife rangers in Nagarhole, Bandipur, and Bhadra tiger reserves use compasses during forest patrol. ISRO's Navigation Centre in Bengaluru uses magnetic and GPS-based navigation for spacecraft guidance. Mangaluru port navigation systems incorporate magnetic compasses as backup instruments.",common_mistakes:"Students say the north pole of a compass needle points to Earth's North Pole \u2014 technically, the compass needle's North pole is attracted to Earth's geographic South magnetic pole (which is near geographic north). However, for school purposes, the standard answer is: the North pole of a magnet/compass points towards geographic north.",memory_tip:"FREE magnet = compass. Suspend any magnet and it shows N\u2013S. Earth is a giant magnet \u2014 it calls all smaller magnets to align with it. You + free magnet = instant compass."},{concept_name:"Making and Losing Magnetism",simple_explanation:"We can make magnets by stroking iron with a magnet or using electric current. Magnets lose their power when heated, dropped hard, or hammered.",detailed_explanation:"Methods to make a magnet: (1) Single-stroke method \u2014 stroke an iron nail repeatedly in one direction with one pole of a bar magnet; after many strokes, the nail becomes a weak magnet. (2) Double-stroke (divided-touch) method \u2014 stroke with both poles simultaneously from the centre outward. (3) Electrical method \u2014 coil copper wire around an iron rod and pass electric current \u2014 this creates an electromagnet, which is magnetic only while current flows. An electromagnet is very useful because it can be switched on and off. Magnets lose their properties when: (a) dropped or struck hard (physical shock disturbs the alignment of magnetic domains), (b) heated strongly (thermal energy randomises domain alignment), (c) stored incorrectly without keepers (soft iron bars placed across poles).",real_life_example:"A scrapyard uses a large electromagnet suspended from a crane \u2014 switch on the current and it lifts tonnes of scrap iron; switch off and the iron drops. MRI machines in hospitals use powerful electromagnets. The electric doorbell and electric motor use electromagnets.",karnataka_example:"Hubballi-Dharwad's industrial zone manufactures small electric motors for agricultural pumps \u2014 all using electromagnet principles. BEML (Bharat Earth Movers Limited) in Bengaluru manufactures earth-moving equipment that uses electromagnets in electric motors. Karnataka's textile looms use electric motors built on electromagnetic principles.",common_mistakes:"Students think once a magnet loses magnetism, it is permanently destroyed. This is not always true \u2014 a magnet made by the single-stroke method can be re-magnetised. Also, an electromagnet is only magnetic when current flows \u2014 students sometimes think it is always a magnet.",memory_tip:"MAKE a magnet: Stroke + Same direction + One pole only. BREAK a magnet: Heat + Hit + Hammer. The three H's destroy magnets: Heat, Hit, Hammer."}],key_vocabulary:[{term:"Magnet",definition:"A material that attracts iron, nickel, and cobalt and has two poles"},{term:"Magnetic material",definition:"A material that is attracted by a magnet \u2014 e.g. iron, nickel, cobalt"},{term:"Non-magnetic material",definition:"A material not attracted by a magnet \u2014 e.g. wood, plastic, copper, aluminium"},{term:"Poles",definition:"The two ends of a magnet (North and South) where magnetic force is strongest"},{term:"Attraction",definition:"The pulling force between unlike poles (N and S) of magnets"},{term:"Repulsion",definition:"The pushing force between like poles (N\u2013N or S\u2013S) of magnets"},{term:"Compass",definition:"A device with a magnetised needle that always points north, used to find direction"},{term:"Lodestone / Magnetite",definition:"A naturally occurring magnetic rock (Fe\u2083O\u2084) \u2014 the first natural magnet discovered"},{term:"Electromagnet",definition:"A temporary magnet made by passing electric current through a coil of wire around an iron core"},{term:"Magnetic field",definition:"The region around a magnet where its magnetic force can be felt"}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Magnetic and Non-magnetic Materials",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name three magnetic materials.",a:"Iron, nickel, and cobalt are the three main magnetic materials."},{type:"Recall",q:"Is aluminium magnetic or non-magnetic?",a:"Aluminium is non-magnetic. It is a metal but is not attracted by a magnet."},{type:"Application",q:"You have a magnet and four objects: a steel spoon, a glass bottle, a copper wire, and an iron nail. Which will be attracted to the magnet?",a:"The steel spoon and the iron nail will be attracted. Glass and copper are non-magnetic."}]},{after_concept:"Poles of a Magnet",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What happens when two North poles of two magnets are brought close?",a:"They repel each other \u2014 like poles repel."},{type:"Understanding",q:"If you cut a bar magnet in half, how many poles will each piece have?",a:"Each half will have two poles \u2014 a North and a South pole. You cannot isolate a single pole."}]}],key_takeaways:["Magnets attract only magnetic materials: iron, nickel, cobalt, and their alloys like steel.","Aluminium, copper, wood, plastic, and rubber are non-magnetic.","Every magnet has two poles: North (N) and South (S). Magnetic force is strongest at the poles.","Like poles repel; unlike poles attract.","A freely suspended magnet always points north\u2013south \u2014 this is the basis of a compass.","Earth behaves like a giant magnet, causing all free magnets to align north\u2013south.","Magnets can be made by stroking iron with a magnet or by using electric current (electromagnet).","Magnets lose their properties when heated, hammered, or dropped hard.","Electromagnets are temporary magnets \u2014 they work only when current flows.","Lodestone (magnetite) is a natural magnet; the legendary discovery involved a shepherd named Magnes."],mind_map:{root:"Fun with Magnets",branches:[{branch:"Materials",sub:["Magnetic: iron, nickel, cobalt, steel","Non-magnetic: wood, plastic, copper, aluminium, glass"]},{branch:"Properties",sub:["Two poles: N and S","Like poles repel","Unlike poles attract","Force strongest at poles"]},{branch:"Direction Finding",sub:["Free magnet \u2192 aligns N\u2013S","Compass = magnetised needle on pivot","Earth = giant magnet"]},{branch:"Making / Losing Magnetism",sub:["Single-stroke method","Electrical method (electromagnet)","Lost by: heat, hammering, dropping"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Ballari iron ore / magnetite deposits","IISc Bengaluru \u2014 materials research","BEML motors (electromagnets)","ISRO navigation sensors"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 13 covers magnetism. Key facts: Magnets attract iron, nickel, and cobalt (INC) \u2014 all other materials including aluminium, copper, and wood are non-magnetic. Every magnet has two poles (North and South); force is strongest at poles. Law: like poles repel, unlike poles attract. A freely suspended magnet aligns N\u2013S because Earth is a giant magnet \u2014 this is the compass principle. Magnets are made by single-stroke method or electrically (electromagnets). Magnets lose power when heated, hammered, or dropped. An electromagnet is active only when electric current flows. Natural magnet: lodestone (magnetite). Karnataka link: Ballari iron ore deposits; BEML and ISRO use electromagnetic technology.",must_remember_facts:["Magnetic materials: iron, nickel, cobalt only (INC). Aluminium and copper are NOT magnetic.","Every magnet has exactly two poles \u2014 North and South. Poles cannot be separated.","Like poles repel; unlike poles attract.","A freely suspended magnet always points north\u2013south.","A compass is a freely suspended magnetised needle used to find direction.","Earth acts like a giant magnet.","Electromagnet = iron core + wire coil + electric current; magnetic ONLY when current flows.","Magnets lose magnetism when heated, hammered, or dropped hard.","Lodestone (magnetite, Fe\u2083O\u2084) is a natural magnet.","Single-stroke method: stroke iron with one pole of a magnet, always in the SAME direction."]}},2537,[]);